e verb needs one or is given twice — which the command line's status constants read and `help` serves (packages/escher-driver/src/schema.rs:436-474). `describe() -> String` (since 2026-10-12-self-description) writes the tool's self-description, the answer of `help`, as one line of JSON through the crate's `json` writer: its keys are `HELP`'s seven result fields in order, every key name read from `HELP.fields` and every text from the tables — `VERBS`, `NODE_FIELDS`, `KEY_NAMES`, `BUSY_CLASSES`, `CAUSES` with their remedies, `EXIT_STATUSES` and the session errors with their remedies: 11 verbs, 13 node keys, 12 key names, 3 busy classes, 9 causes, 4 exit statuses, 15 session errors — reading no environment and printing nothing; its callers are the command line's `help` and the MCP server's `help` tool, which serve the same bytes — the program's own fixed `'static` text of the one table riding the ratified carriers, ratified by the founder (2026-10-12, «Внутри, с правилом», by question dialog in the overseer session, relayed verbatim by the overseer) (packages/escher-driver/src/describe.rs:11-58). A step that ran and did not go quiet is a result — `settled: false` with its class — not a refusal. `advance` moves the session's clock forward by `ms` milliseconds on every session — the engine's animations, the overlay scrollbar fade and, through the step a caller hands over with `Session::with_time` where there is one, the app's own time together — then settles; two `click`s on one point with no `advance` of at least 500 ms between them read as a double click, as `click`'s help says (ratified by the founder, 2026-10-18). `validate(call: &Call) -> Result<Command, Refusal>` takes a `Call { verb: String, args: Vec<(String, ArgValue)> }` (`ArgValue` is `Text(String)` · `Number(i64)` · `Flag(bool)`) and receives no `Session`, so a refused call changes nothing; it answers once per call, the first failure in this order: the verb by exact name (absent → `unknown-verb`; so does a se