d` · `OutOfBound`, a variant that names an argument holding the schema's own name for it; none of the three has a `String` field, so a refusal holds nothing a call supplied. All eight are returned by code: `validate` returns `unknown-verb` and `malformed` only, and the executor returns the other six — `not-found`, `stale`, `disabled`, `covered`, `off-screen` and `fonts-unavailable`, the last from `screenshot` alone, checked before anything is painted (below). Two texts were reworded at 2026-10-07-refusal-detection, both `off-screen`'s and each on a recorded word, no other cause text moving: its meaning reads "the element lies outside the viewport, or outside the visible part of a scrolling box that holds it; a scrolling box under a transform is read where it stands untransformed, so an element inside it can read `off-screen` though it shows" — its first clause the operator's (2026-10-07, at that chunk's plan review), its second a measured limit (the Scrolling, selection, tree contract) (packages/escher-driver/src/refusal.rs:25-28; packages/escher-driver/src/refusal.rs:81-86) and its remedy "the element is out of view: bring it into view with `scroll`, then act again" (the founder, 2026-10-07, relayed verbatim by the overseer). At 2026-10-10-driver-cli four more texts were amended so that the tool itself said two measured limits and what `type` does (the operator, 2026-10-10, at that chunk's plan review: a measured limit is stated where an agent reads the tool), and at 2026-10-10-scrolling-box-bounds-and-hit, both limits fixed in the engine, three of the four were restated, the cause set — eight, in its order — and every other text standing: `covered`'s meaning reads "another element is hit at the point the action would land; a box clipped on one axis stops a hit at its whole padding box, so an element lying outside it along the axis it does not clip can read `covered` though it shows; a box with no width or no height is hit at its centre on the element that holds it